ich embrace three thousa...\"\n  1390→      ],\n  1391→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  1392→      \"ipa\": null,\n  1393→      \"phonetic_spelling\": null,\n  1394→      \"audio_url\": null,\n  1395→      \"user_pronunciation\": null,\n  1396→      \"notes\": \"Multiple pronunciations: vegetables (PROH-doos); to make (pruh-DOOS)\"\n  1397→    },\n  1398→    {\n  1399→      \"word\": \"deliberate\",\n  1400→      \"flag_reason\": \"homograph\",\n  1401→      \"occurrences\": 1,\n  1402→      \"first_position\": 11611,\n  1403→      \"chapter_indices\": [\n  1404→        1\n  1405→      ],\n  1406→      \"context_examples\": [\n  1407→        \"...his group in the direction of the intruder, who, at the moment was also near at hand, and now, with deliberate and stately step, made closer approach to the speaker. But from a certain nameless awe with which t...\"\n  1408→      ],\n  1409→      \"confidence\": \"high\",\n  1410→      \"ipa\": null,\n  1411→      \"phonetic_spelling\": null,\n  1412→      \"audio_url\": null,\n  1413→      \"user_pronunciation\": null,\n  1414→      \"notes\": \"Multiple pronunciations: adj (di-LIB-er-it); verb (di-LIB-er-ayt)\"\n  1415→    }\n  1416→  ],\n  1417→  \"glossary\": null,\n  1418→  \"overview\": {\n  1419→    \"structure\": {\n  1420→      \"chapter_count\": 1,\n  1421→      \"has_titles\": false,\n  1422→      \"has_parts\": false,\n  1423→      \"narrative_style\": \"unknown\",\n  1424→      \"description\": \"This book contains 1 chapters. Chapters are numbered sequentially.\"\n  1425→    },\n  1426→    \"plot_summary\": {\n  1427→      \"plot_summary\": \"The story begins in the midst of a terrifying pandemic known as the 'Red Death,' a merciless plague characterized by bleeding from the pores and swift, agonizing death. Fleeing the devastation outside, Prince Prospero, a man of immense wealth and arrogance, retreats with a thousand courtiers into a secluded, heavily fortified abbey, a fortress of luxury and isolation. Within its opulent walls, he orchestrates a lavish masked ball, designed to distract from the horrors beyond. The abbey is divided into seven uniquely decorated rooms, each with stained-glass windows matching their color scheme, all illuminated by flickering firelight from tripods. The final, black chamber, adorned with blood-red windows and dominated by a massive ebony clock, stands apart—its hourly chime halting the revelry and casting a pall of dread over the festivities.\\n\\nAs the night progresses, the mood darkens with each chime of the clock, a reminder of time’s inexorable march and the ever-present threat of death. At midnight, a mysterious figure appears—dressed as the Red Death itself, with a corpse-like mask and garments stained with blood. The figure moves silently through the seven rooms, inciting terror among the guests, who are paralyzed by fear. Prince Prospero, consumed by fury and a sense of personal affront, draws his dagger and pursues the intruder through each chamber, driven by a need to assert control and restore order. His pursuit becomes a desperate, symbolic journey through the stages of life and death, culminating in the black chamber.\\n\\nIn the final room, the prince confronts the figure, only to collapse dead upon realizing the truth: the figure is not a man, but an empty shell, a physical manifestation of the plague he had tried so desperately to escape. The revelation is immediate and fatal—every guest, including Prospero, succumbs to the Red Death, as the plague infiltrates the abbey from within. The lights go out, the music ceases, and all life is extinguished. The story ends with the complete annihilation of the revelers, underscoring the futility of attempting to evade death through wealth, isolation, or spectacle. The final image is one of absolute stillness and finality, where even the most elaborate defenses cannot withstand the inevitability of mortality.\",\n  1428→      \"themes\": [\n  1429→        \"identity\",\n  1430→ 